<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! NGINX and Caddy ~ commodity bodies for one Pronaos

> Status: RESEARCHED DESIGN — no reverse-proxy replacement, package, or Compose integration stands here.
> NGINX and Caddy can carry a Pronaos. The carrier changes; the Pronaos contract does not.

The Pronaos names one Herm/Lararium composition's intimate first face. NGINX and Caddy already supply mature
HTTP serving, TLS termination, static-byte delivery, reverse forwarding, logs, and WebSocket transport. They do
not need a DreamNet replacement. A generated or hand-held carrier configuration realizes the Pronaos route
receipt while the house keeps identity, documents, CAS, VEIL scope, Oracle, merge, and admission authority.

The production direction remains a composed public Web shore plus a live Node shore under one operator-held
arrival name. Node and Web remain peers with differentiated capability stacks. The carrier creates no client/server
ontology; it provides an ordinary delivery body for a peer composition.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/related-carriers>>

!! Related carriers ~ the carrier study rides existing law

The common first-face contract lives in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos">>. Browser origin
and DNS/ACME pressure lives in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context">>. Reach-plane custody
and foreign transport remain in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/reach-plane">>. The active work handoff
and its red-first ordering live in <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/crew-handoff-codex">>.

This meme adds carrier pattern knowledge. It does not replace those carriers or silently open their unresolved
operator rulings.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/myth-and-boundary>>

!! Myth and boundary ~ the carrier wears the forecourt's clothes

```text
Compitum   roads meet
Herm       carriage and public crossing stand
Pronaos    this house presents its declared first face
Carrier    NGINX, Caddy, cloud ingress, or another delivery body realizes that face
Lararium   persona, owned documents, held local material, and house authority remain within
Librarium   deferred public crossroads of books, images, media, and offered editions
```

The carrier holds a deployment receipt and a finite public route inventory. It may serve a prepared Web artifact,
seed, seed-named CAS members, public FLOW, or a declared live introduction. It never receives a general Lararium
root, private bag mount, persona key, peer-identity authority, or arbitrary CAS path.

The carrier's TLS certificate proves an origin to a browser only. The domain remains a rendezvous hint. The peer
key proves peer identity. The route selects delivery. A capability permits an action. No relation silently grants
the next one.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/common-contract>>

!! Common contract ~ translate the same Pronaos receipt into either carrier

The Pronaos descriptor carries finite, inspectable facts:

```text
origin / name binding             local DNS, contracted domain, or LAN name
public artifact inventory         exact index, workers, assets, and integrity receipts
bootstrap inventory               exact seed.json plus seed-named CID-checked members
public route inventory             exact paths, methods, response classes, and refusal shape
transport introduction            one bounded live target, if the operator declares one
delivery policy                   CSP, SRI where applicable, cache, CORS, and headers
trusted-hop policy                which carrier addresses may supply forwarded context
process attribution               which prepared shore owns each response
```

The descriptor must compile or be reviewed before the carrier starts. A route never means "serve this directory",
"read any CID", or "fall through to the application". The public root remains a dedicated read-only projection,
separate from private bags, persona stores, Oracle files, and the full house CAS. A symlink or broad mounted root
must not smuggle an inner house through a public file handler.

Integrity addressing and delivery caching remain separate fields in the receipt. A seed, index, or CAS member may
carry an integrity digest and a route witness while receiving `no-store`; content addressing makes wrong bytes
detectable, but it does not prescribe a cache lifetime or revalidation policy. The carrier applies the declared
cache class route by route.

The carrier must return one outward refusal shape for missing, private, traversal, and undeclared paths, so the
arrival surface does not become a private-object existence oracle. A known public integrity mismatch names an
integrity failure. A known live target loss names temporary unavailability. Detailed diagnostics remain local to
the operator and test witness.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cache-law>>

!! Cache law ~ integrity witnesses and delivery classes never fuse

The current Node public byte witness demonstrates the separation concretely. Its exact index route `/` and exact
`/genesis/seed.json` route answer `no-store`, while selected exact `/assets/<name>` and `/genesis/cas/<cid>` routes
answer `public, immutable, max-age=31536000`. Those response classes come from the route adapter's explicit policy,
not from the fact that a byte or CID can be hashed. The adapter's tests assert the distinction.

The bulb and Oracle read-faces carry the same split: pointers and manifests answer `no-store`; selected snapshot or
blob routes may answer `public, immutable` when their serving face declares that class. A future Pronaos descriptor
must preserve the field rather than deriving it from `seed.json`, CID shape, or a filename.

```text
integrity receipt       wrong bytes can be detected
cache class             carrier may retain or revalidate a response
freshness observation   a local delivery fact, never causal order
authority               a held capability, never a cache consequence
```

The first carrier witness must assert both dimensions independently:

* `/` and `genesis/seed.json` return exact bytes plus `no-store`;
* a selected hashed asset or CAS member returns its declared cache class;
* a route declared `no-store` stays `no-store` through NGINX and Caddy;
* changing a cache header never changes CID/hash verification;
* a cache hit never expands the route inventory or private read scope;
* an integrity mismatch never becomes a cache miss that silently falls through to another face.

The implementation may later select different cache classes for the same kind of byte under a different operator
policy. The receipt and witness must say which policy stands. No global clock decides any of these classes.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/nginx>>

!! NGINX ~ explicit glass and mature operations

NGINX gives an operator a deliberately explicit configuration language and a mature serving/proxy process. It
serves static files from a configured root, terminates TLS with explicitly named certificates and keys, forwards
selected routes, and emits access/error records. Its WebSocket tunnel requires explicit HTTP/1.1 upgrade handling;
its default `proxy_read_timeout` closes an idle upstream after 60 seconds unless the operator chooses a different
transport policy or the peer sends keepalive traffic.

Official pattern receipts:

* HTTPS certificate/key configuration: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/configuring_https_servers.html
* static roots and `try_files`: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html
* WebSocket upgrade and idle timeout: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/websocket.html
* proxy headers and read timeout: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html

NGINX's operational virtues:

* explicit certificate custody and renewal integration;
* broad adoption, stable deployment knowledge, and many existing observability paths;
* clear separation between static locations and bounded upstream locations;
* a good fit where an organization already runs NGINX and wants the Pronaos descriptor translated into its
  existing edge configuration;
* a good fit for a regional carrier fleet where operators already review configuration, reloads, and logs.

Its costs:

* ACME issuance and renewal need a separate, persisted tool or operator workflow;
* WebSocket headers, trusted forwarding, timeout, and reload behavior require deliberate configuration;
* a large configuration surface can hide an accidental broad root, fallback, or proxy route;
* certificate and generated-route custody need explicit runbook and aftermath witnesses.

The NGINX reference shape remains schematic until the red witness owns it:

```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name house.example;
    ssl_certificate     /run/pronaos/cert.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /run/pronaos/key.pem;

    # generated exact public locations; never the Lararium root
    location = / { try_files /index.html =404; root /srv/pronaos/public; }
    location = /genesis/seed.json { try_files /genesis/seed.json =404; root /srv/pronaos/public; }
    location /live/ { proxy_pass http://lararium-node:8787; }
}
```

The example names the shape, not permission to copy it unchanged. Generated locations need exact inventory,
explicit headers, `Upgrade`/`Connection` handling for the live route, and a trusted-hop policy. A broad `location /`
or SPA fallback would violate this meme's refusal law.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/caddy>>

!! Caddy ~ approachable glass with automatic HTTPS

Caddy gives an operator a human-oriented Caddyfile and a native JSON configuration/API. Its automatic HTTPS
provisions and renews certificates for qualifying public names, redirects HTTP to HTTPS by default, and creates a
local CA for local names when the operator arranges client trust. Its `reverse_proxy` supports WebSocket upgrade and
bidirectional tunneling. Its `file_server` serves a configured filesystem and supports explicit path matchers,
headers, logs, and metrics.

Official pattern receipts:

* automatic HTTPS and persistent certificate data: https://caddyserver.com/docs/automatic-https
* reverse proxy, WebSocket, streaming, and reload behavior:
  https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/directives/reverse_proxy
* static file serving and its root/symlink warning:
  https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/directives/file_server
* trusted proxy ranges and forwarded headers: https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/options
* Caddyfile route and handler ordering: https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/concepts

Caddy's operational virtues:

* a household can name a domain and receive ordinary ACME HTTPS without separately wiring a renewal daemon;
* a concise configuration suits one laptop, a small household server, or an operator who wants a readable first
  deployment;
* reverse proxy, static delivery, response headers, access logs, and WebSocket handling live in one carrier;
* a native API and JSON model can later receive a generated Pronaos descriptor without forcing a hand-edited file.

Its costs:

* ACME and local-CA state require a durable, protected data directory; a disposable container loses the carrier's
  renewal and local trust state;
* local HTTPS still needs client trust for Caddy's local CA; it does not dissolve the secure-context wall by itself;
* a public DNS name, reachable ports, and DNS control still remain prerequisites for ordinary public ACME;
* `file_server` does not create a security boundary: symbolic links can reach outside a root, so the public
  projection must remain a dedicated tree with no private links;
* Caddy's automatic defaults can conceal policy unless the operator records the adapted configuration and tests the
  exact generated routes, headers, and refusal behavior.

The reference shape remains schematic:

```caddyfile
house.example {
    root * /srv/pronaos/public
    @arrival path / /assets/exact-worker.js /genesis/seed.json /genesis/cas/<cid>
    handle @arrival {
        file_server
    }
    @live path /live/<declared>
    reverse_proxy @live lararium-node:8787
}
```

The public tree remains a prepared, read-only projection. No `browse`, broad root, `try_files` equivalent, or SPA
fallback enters the production contract without a named route witness. The Caddyfile syntax supplies a carrier
shape; the Pronaos descriptor still supplies positive route ownership, cache classes, trusted hops, and refusal.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/pattern-integrity-rhymes>>

!! Pattern-integrity rhymes ~ borrow the body, preserve the law

''Commodity carrier, sovereign application.'' NGINX and Caddy move bytes and forward requests. The served peer
still verifies its own peer key and capability relation. A reverse proxy never becomes the remote peer merely because
it holds a socket to that peer.

''Name, origin, identity, delivery, authority.'' A DNS answer gives rendezvous. TLS gives a browser an origin
boundary. A key proves identity. A route selects bytes. A capability permits action. NGINX's certificate and Caddy's
ACME state therefore remain origin-attestation material, never peer identity.

''Content address, then choose cache.'' A seed, index, or seed-named member may receive an integrity receipt while
remaining `no-store`. A route may explicitly grant a selected immutable cache class to bytes whose serving contract
allows it; content addressing alone never grants that class. Selectors, discovery, and mutable public declarations
usually need explicit revalidation or `no-store`. Cache presence never proves membership, freshness truth, authority,
or causal order.

''Trusted forward, never forwarded trust.'' Both carriers can pass host, scheme, and client headers. The Node shore
must trust those fields only from configured carrier hops, never from the incoming visitor. A caller-supplied
`X-Forwarded-*` value must not rewrite the peer's self-URL, origin, or capability decision.

''Transport introduction, then peer handshake.'' A browser `Origin` check protects a browser-facing WebSocket path.
It does not authenticate a non-browser peer. The served vessel still performs its own key handshake and
capability/admission decision after the carrier introduces the transport.

''Local continuity, remote reach.'' A home carrier can disappear while leaf vessels retain local causal state and
previously acquired public bytes. A regional Nexus can relay or cache selected public material without becoming a
mandatory host or planetary authority. WAN recovery carries a later crossing; it does not rewrite the island.

''Generated receipt, not magic config.'' The operator's route receipt should render to NGINX, Caddy, cloud ingress,
or another carrier while preserving the same positive route inventory and red witnesses. The carrier choice remains
deployment custody, not a protocol version gate or a hidden identity translation.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/selection>>

!! Selection ~ choose the carrier by operator context, not mythology

| deployment context | first carrier lean | reason | watch |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| one developer laptop | direct process, Caddy optional | shortest arrival experiment | localhost secure-context exception does not prove LAN HTTPS |
| household laptop, NAS, or mini-server | Caddy | readable config and automatic public/local HTTPS path | persist ACME/local-CA data; keep public tree narrow |
| household already running NGINX | NGINX | reuse trusted operations and explicit certificate custody | add ACME renewal, WebSocket, forwarding, and timeout witnesses |
| small organization | existing NGINX or Caddy fleet | preserve local operations and one descriptor per house/domain | review generated routes, logs, secrets, and reload behavior |
| regional Nexus | NGINX, Caddy, Envoy, or managed ingress | carrier fleet can relay/cache selected projections | Nexus remains a causal island and never owns member Lararia |
| NAT-bound household | either carrier plus an outbound relay/tunnel | carrier handles local origin; relay handles reach | end-to-end peer key and capability proofs survive relay |
| solar/planetary federation | many local carriers and Nexuses | no universal front door or global clock | discovery and relay remain hints and bounded capabilities |

The alpha ruling should keep both NGINX and Caddy as supported carrier patterns in the design, while choosing one
reference witness at implementation time. Caddy reads more pono for a first household path because automatic HTTPS
and a readable file/proxy surface shorten the ceremony. NGINX reads more pono for an existing civic operations shore
because explicit config, mature fleet practice, and separate certificate custody make the carrier's behavior easier to
review. Neither choice deserves a new package until carrier emitters and lifecycle tests develop their own substantial
surface.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/deployment-scales>>

!! Deployment scales ~ one contract, many physical bodies

''One household.'' A laptop or NAS runs a Node peer with durable private volumes and a carrier with a read-only
Pronaos projection. Family leaf vessels reach a LAN name or contracted domain. WAN loss leaves the local house and
already acquired public bytes usable.

''Small organization.'' Several house peers share one carrier or several named Pronaea. Each Node keeps its own
causal island, persona material, and document authority. The carrier exposes only the operator's declared public
shore and bounded live introductions.

''Regional Nexus.'' A regional mesh offers discovery hints, relay, and selected public cache. Member Lararia keep
their own keys and local state. A regional carrier may lose reach without deciding a member's identity, membership,
merge, or document truth.

''Planetary/solar DreamNet.'' Many regional islands compose through declared crossings. No global carrier, route,
wall clock, or merge authority needs to exist. A name may move or lapse; the peer key and local causal history stay
with the vessel that holds them.

The physical stack can read:

```text
carrier container / host process
  ├─ read-only Pronaos public projection
  └─ bounded internal route to Node peer

Node peer container / host process
  ├─ durable local causal state
  ├─ private bags, persona, Oracle, and local CAS
  └─ peer-key and capability decisions

leaf vessels
  ├─ own keys and local islands
  └─ public projections and explicitly crossed relations
```

The carrier never mounts the whole Lararium. The Node peer never needs public port exposure when the carrier can
forward a bounded internal target. A tunnel or relay may alter reachability and never receives the inner house's
authority by that fact.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/gaia-elyncia>>

!! Operator stories ~ Gaia and Elyncia

**Operators on Gaia, Earth circa 2026, SHALL need** to raise a household Lararium from a laptop, NAS, or small
server behind a familiar carrier without giving DNS, TLS, or cloud operations custody of their documents, identities,
or local causal state.

**Operators on Gaia SHALL need** a readable first deployment with secure browser boot, offline household continuity,
explicit LAN/public-name reach, and a path to civic-scale operations that preserves the same route and capability law.

**Operators on Elyncia, the Orichalcum Mythpunk RPG, SHALL need** a shrine whose forecourt can take the bodies
already available in the world — household carrier, district edge, relay, regional Nexus — while the shrine's
persona, archives, and local memories remain its own.

**Operators on Elyncia SHALL need** a waystone that can vanish, move, or change keeper without changing the identity
and causal history of the vessel that crossed it. A carrier offers the bearing; the traveler keeps their vessel.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/red-witness>>

!! Red witness ~ prove the contract through both bodies

The first witness should compile one finite route receipt to one carrier at a time. It must attribute `/`, one
worker, `genesis/seed.json`, and one seed-named CAS member to the named public shore; compare every byte against the
expected receipt; assert each route's declared cache class; and prove that the live route reaches only the named
Node peer. The current adapter's positive matrix gives `/` and `genesis/seed.json` `no-store`, while selected worker
and CAS members receive `public, immutable, max-age=31536000`; the Pronaos receipt may choose another class, but
the witness must name it rather than infer it from a digest.

Each weakening must read red:

* mount the Lararium root or a broad CAS root into the carrier;
* request an undeclared path, CID, or method;
* return an SPA index for a missing worker, seed, CAS member, or private route;
* substitute wrong bytes beneath a valid-looking CID;
* accept arbitrary forwarded host, scheme, or client headers;
* treat TLS certificate, DNS name, carrier socket, or cache hit as peer identity;
* treat an integrity digest as permission to add `immutable` or remove `no-store`;
* let a carrier rewrite a declared cache class, or let a cache hit answer an undeclared/private route;
* accept a disallowed browser `Origin` or skip the later peer-key handshake;
* let NGINX's idle timeout or Caddy's reload behavior silently redefine a live crossing;
* lose Caddy's certificate data or NGINX's renewal state and call the carrier healthy;
* let a carrier outage become a successful private fallback;
* use a wall-clock value as authority, shared order, membership, freshness truth, or merge choice.

The same positive and deliberate-weakening suite must run against a direct Node shore, a separate static shore,
NGINX composition, Caddy composition, and later any managed edge. The physical carrier may change; the witness
must preserve route ownership, integrity, refusal, peer symmetry, local continuity, and named loss behavior.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/open-and-deferred>>

!! Open and deferred ~ keep the cut small

* The first implementation carrier remains an operator ruling: Caddy for the household reference or NGINX for an
  existing civic operations reference. The common descriptor and witness come first.
* ACME/DNS delegation, split-horizon DNS, certificate renewal, and origin-attestation custody remain a distinct
  reach-plane wave. A carrier can enact those policies but cannot decide their DreamNet authority.
* NAT traversal may use an outbound relay or tunnel. The relay carries sealed transport and never becomes a peer,
  Lararium, Librarium, or regional authority.
* The public Librarium remains deferred. A carrier may later carry a deliberately offered Librarium projection; no
  carrier gets to expose the house-held `library:` collection by default.
* Android, iOS, Mudlet, Unreal, and future vessels consume the same Pronaos route and verification intent through
  their own capability adapters. No carrier gets to flatten those vessel differences into a user/server story.
* A dedicated `@lararium/pronaos` package remains unnecessary until descriptor emitters and carrier lifecycle tests
  gain a genuinely independent surface. Begin in `@lararium/mesh`, with Node/Web/Browser adapters where their caps
  already live.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/sources>>

!! Sources ~ engineering receipts, not imported authority

* NGINX HTTPS: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/configuring_https_servers.html
* NGINX WebSocket proxying: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/websocket.html
* NGINX proxy module: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html
* NGINX core/static paths: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html
* Caddy automatic HTTPS: https://caddyserver.com/docs/automatic-https
* Caddy reverse proxy and WebSocket transport: https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/directives/reverse_proxy
* Caddy static files and symlink boundary: https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/directives/file_server
* Caddy trusted proxies: https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/options
* Caddy route/config concepts: https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/concepts
* RFC 7239 forwarded headers: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7239.html
* W3C Secure Contexts: https://www.w3.org/TR/secure-contexts/
* W3C Subresource Integrity: https://www.w3.org/TR/SRI/
* Ink & Switch local-first: https://www.inkandswitch.com/essay/local-first/

These sources describe carrier mechanics and local-first pattern rhymes. They do not choose a DreamNet package,
grant a capability, establish peer identity, or create a compatibility/version gate.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/aftercare>>

!! Aftercare ~ Evergreen and L-Prime

The early-alpha tree carries no outside consumer who needs a compatibility alias. When implementation lands, stale
carrier experiments, broad roots, copied configs, and unused proxy helpers should retire in the same aftermath wave
that proves the surviving descriptor. Keep one readable contract and one receipt per active carrier witness.

The next integration wave should update the Pronaos roadmap with the carrier choice, red witness, generated-config
receipt, and loss matrix. A cleanup wave must re-scan code, Compose, docs, and memes for stale bootstrap-shore wording
that accidentally names a Librarium rather than the Pronaos byte-exposure projection. The Librarium itself remains
deferred and does not enter this carrier meme by implication.

<<~/ahu>>

